        ":question/id": "e0473c92-6bbb-51d0-9d33-3793374dc209",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-350414",
        ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$"
      },
